(799856, 85526, 9411, '<td valign="top">Just saw your thread... My comments are, of course, just my opinion.<br/> <br/>First let me say, she is stunning and I think this is a very good image.<br/> <br/>My suggestions:<br/>I think the light is a bit harsh andcould have benefited from some selective diffusion.  <br/> <br/>Is that her arm?  Is she holding her arms above her head? I''m not sure why you chose to pose her that way.  <br/> <br/>A simple head study would have been stunning. If you were trying to use her clothing as an element, I would suggest having a second dress (?) draped as a background some distance away from your subject. Letting us focus on her facial details. <br/> <br/>I feel the light being warm is a very good choice.  The small bit of background that is grey, does not go with that theme though.<br/> <br/>Still a very nice shot with a lot of potential.</td>', 'Just saw your thread... My comments are, of course, just my opinion. First let me say, she is stunning and I think this is a very good image. My suggestions:I think the light is a bit harsh andcould have benefited from some selective diffusion.   Is that her arm?  Is she holding her arms above her head? I''m not sure why you chose to pose her that way.   A simple head study would have been stunning. If you were trying to use her clothing as an element, I would suggest having a second dress (?) draped as a background some distance away from your subject. Letting us focus on her facial details.  I feel the light being warm is a very good choice.  The small bit of background that is grey, does not go with that theme though. Still a very nice shot with a lot of potential.', '2004-07-07 10:16:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(800013, 86099, 22545, '<td valign="top">This is a stunning portrait! I am surprised it didn''t place first.  Absolutely beautiful.</td>', 'This is a stunning portrait! I am surprised it didn''t place first.  Absolutely beautiful.', '2004-07-07 12:09:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(800063, 85265, 22619, '<td valign="top">Thanks to everyone for their comments. This was my first challenge, and I have to say I am already learning from the experience and from the comments received.<br/> <br/>I am going to reshoot this picture (of my daughter, who is 8 by the way) trying to take into account many of the suggestions given. While not new to photography in general, I am new to really taking it seriously, and I''m hoping participating in these challenges and interacting with members of this website will improve my skills.<br/> <br/>Thanks again!<br/> <br/>Tim</td>', 'Thanks to everyone for their comments. This was my first challenge, and I have to say I am already learning from the experience and from the comments received. I am going to reshoot this picture (of my daughter, who is 8 by the way) trying to take into account many of the suggestions given. While not new to photography in general, I am new to really taking it seriously, and I''m hoping participating in these challenges and interacting with members of this website will improve my skills. Thanks again! Tim', '2004-07-07 12:39:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(800259, 85934, 22734, '<td valign="top">There is a story behind this shot. I sailed with captain George in Maryland. He is tan brown all over with shiny skin. When he hoisted the main sail, with the sun at 3:00 o''clock and the reflected light off the sail lit his shadow area with wonderful highlights, I decided to duplicate this effect with a studio shot, hence the name Capt. George Highlighted.<br/>  I used strobes with umbrellas and then employed a fresnel spot light, I knew the spotlight would get me in trouble because some photographers do not like the face sculptured with highlights, but I was following the feel when he was on the deck and I took over 40 shots, some simply perfect but lacking the highlights. The item he leans on is his captains'' jacket. This inclusion was also questionable, but I went with it anyway.<br/>  Captain George is the nicest guy. He virtually feeds everyone in the marina and his disposition is radiant and uplifting.</td>', 'There is a story behind this shot. I sailed with captain George in Maryland. He is tan brown all over with shiny skin. When he hoisted the main sail, with the sun at 3:00 o''clock and the reflected light off the sail lit his shadow area with wonderful highlights, I decided to duplicate this effect with a studio shot, hence the name Capt. George Highlighted.  I used strobes with umbrellas and then employed a fresnel spot light, I knew the spotlight would get me in trouble because some photographers do not like the face sculptured with highlights, but I was following the feel when he was on the deck and I took over 40 shots, some simply perfect but lacking the highlights. The item he leans on is his captains'' jacket. This inclusion was also questionable, but I went with it anyway.  Captain George is the nicest guy. He virtually feeds everyone in the marina and his disposition is radiant and uplifting.', '2004-07-07 14:31:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(800522, 86231, 20577, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by grigrigirl:</b></div><hr/><i> it is shallow beauty. I entered it because I was curious of my color ability and my monitor calibration in relation to everyone else''s. I entered it as a last minute whim. This image is odd and I cant quite put my finger on it..but I think it is the lack of depth and soul. She was nothing but pose...I was unable to pull anything out of her. I did not neat image or overly process her skin. It is not that her skin looks plastic but maybe it is she who is plastic?...pondering and scratching my chin... </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thank you for answering my question. I was hoping you wouldn''t take it to wrong way. I just wanted to get inside your mind a little. <br/> <br/>I think the one thing that strikes me odd about the picture is her smile... it seems that she is faking it. <br/> <br/></td>', 'Originally posted by grigrigirl: it is shallow beauty. I entered it because I was curious of my color ability and my monitor calibration in relation to everyone else''s. I entered it as a last minute whim. This image is odd and I cant quite put my finger on it..but I think it is the lack of depth and soul. She was nothing but pose...I was unable to pull anything out of her. I did not neat image or overly process her skin. It is not that her skin looks plastic but maybe it is she who is plastic?...pondering and scratching my chin...   Thank you for answering my question. I was hoping you wouldn''t take it to wrong way. I just wanted to get inside your mind a little.  I think the one thing that strikes me odd about the picture is her smile... it seems that she is faking it.  ', '2004-07-07 16:42:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(800564, 85934, 1031, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by graphicfunk:</b></div><hr/><i>Captain George is the nicest guy. He virtually feeds everyone in the marina and his disposition is radiant and uplifting. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Be sure to tell him "thanks for sharing ..." from all of us here.</td>', 'Originally posted by graphicfunk:Captain George is the nicest guy. He virtually feeds everyone in the marina and his disposition is radiant and uplifting.  Be sure to tell him "thanks for sharing ..." from all of us here.', '2004-07-07 17:08:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(800598, 86212, 22734, '<td valign="top">from the critique club:<br/> First: the composition is strong excepting the ambiguity of the angle. Just a little more to the right to concentrate on the profile. The lighting is very nice and gentle and suiting the pensive mood you are portraying. Minor improvements include asking the model to wet lips just before shooting. Personally, I would have requested a slight curl of the slip to suggest an incipient smile. Not complete, not halfway. The very start. However, this would make it a different portrait. Placing subject just further from background would have also elimitated the shadow detail at bottom of head. Since you cropped a part of the head, the eye will linger at the visible part.<br/>    The major fault with this image is its color balance. Whenever a portrait is done you will find color differences between face and neck and shoulders and arms. The best advise I can give is to opt for a slight desaturation and make certain you have a white balance reference. For example, the red and yellow have crept into the eyes. This is more prevalent when the shot is done with incandescent light.<br/>   Since this is your first attempt, rest assured that you did very well for you managed to introduce character and the idea for the angle was good.</td>', 'from the critique club: First: the composition is strong excepting the ambiguity of the angle. Just a little more to the right to concentrate on the profile. The lighting is very nice and gentle and suiting the pensive mood you are portraying. Minor improvements include asking the model to wet lips just before shooting. Personally, I would have requested a slight curl of the slip to suggest an incipient smile. Not complete, not halfway. The very start. However, this would make it a different portrait. Placing subject just further from background would have also elimitated the shadow detail at bottom of head. Since you cropped a part of the head, the eye will linger at the visible part.    The major fault with this image is its color balance. Whenever a portrait is done you will find color differences between face and neck and shoulders and arms. The best advise I can give is to opt for a slight desaturation and make certain you have a white balance reference. For example, the red and yellow have crept into the eyes. This is more prevalent when the shot is done with incandescent light.   Since this is your first attempt, rest assured that you did very well for you managed to introduce character and the idea for the angle was good.', '2004-07-07 17:29:19.000000', NULL, 0),
